From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hunter B Hough <git@hough.ink>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 22:49:08 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 30/38] fix(gastown): stop the deacon's push-state sweep from
 gating session start (gcp-oo0v)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The deacon could not start. `dolt-push-state-check.sh --snapshot` was wired as
a BLOCKING pre_start in agents/deacon/agent.toml; measured by hand on the
affected host it takes 18.565s (exit 0) against gascity's 10s [session]
setup_timeout. gc SIGKILLs a pre_start on that deadline and a killed pre_start
fails the WHOLE session start, so the deacon died on every cycle, six failures
in an hour latched the supervisor circuit breaker, and town patrols were dead
from 2026-08-14T20:10:40Z. The script was never broken — a sweep that issues
one `gc bd sql -C <scope>` per scope simply cannot fit a start-up budget at
this city's ~5-8s per-gc-call cost, and no budget flag would save it (gcp-uzq0
shows the budget approach starves here).

So the sweep moves to where it has time rather than being made smaller. The
deacon carries no pre_start at all; mol-deacon-patrol's `dolt-push-divergence`
step now runs the detector itself on the patrol cadence, which also means the
alert reads a FRESH measurement instead of one taken once at session start.
The step resolves the script from the formula's OWN resolved source path via
`gc formula list --json` — pack assets live in a content-hashed cache a formula
step cannot name, and the formula's sibling copy is the one candidate
guaranteed present and version-coherent with it (a formula from pin X can never
reach a detector from pin Y). GC_PACK_DIR is a fallback only; depending on it
alone is what made the refinery's approval gate permanently unresolvable
(gcp-amo). The old snapshot fallback is gone: nothing writes that file at start
any more, and a stale reading presented as this cycle's is worse than silence,
so an unresolvable detector is now reported as BLIND rather than logged as OK.
`--snapshot` survives as an on-demand operator artifact.

This is the SECOND instance of the class — gcp-ntbf was the witness reaper,
same shape, different script — and both arrived unnoticed on a pin bump rather
than from anyone editing an agent. So the durable part of this change is the
pack-wide guard, tests/test_agent_pre_start_budget.sh: an INVENTORY of every
pre_start in the repo, parsed from the TOML rather than grepped, so a new one
fails the test until somebody measures it. It also asserts each inventoried
script still DEFINES run_bounded/budget_left (not merely mentions them — a
deleted helper with live call sites is how a bound rots away invisibly), that
no remote-facing git call in those scripts is missing a deadline, and that each
declared default budget is numerically inside setup_timeout, since "raise it to
30s so it stops timing out" is the shape of the next incident.

That guard found a third latent instance: worktree-setup.sh, the polecat and
refinery pre_start, ran `git fetch` and `git pull --rebase` with no deadline at
all. Whitelisting it would have built the hole straight into the guard, so its
network calls now share a 6s budget (GC_WORKTREE_SETUP_BUDGET_SECONDS) and are
abandoned rather than retried when it expires — a worktree one fetch behind is
fixed next cycle; a session that never starts is not. Interrupting a rebase
this way is strictly gentler than the status quo, where gc SIGKILLs the whole
group at setup_timeout with no signal the child can act on. The local git
plumbing is deliberately left unbudgeted: it is disk-bound and it is the reason
the script exists.

Validation. All 10 gastown/tests/*.sh pass, including the updated
test_dolt_push_outage_detection_is_wired, which now asserts the inverse of what
it asserted before (it was the RED test at the start of this change: "deacon
must run the auto-push detector from pre_start"). The new guard was
mutation-tested against the three regressions it exists to catch — re-adding
the deacon's pre_start, raising a budget past setup_timeout, and removing the
bound from worktree-setup.sh both by deleting the helper and by unwrapping a
single fetch — and it fails on all three with the incident named in the
message. worktree-setup.sh was smoke-tested end to end against a real
origin (create, idempotent re-run, no --sync, invalid budget rejected), and
its cutoff verified against a non-routable remote on both the coreutils
`timeout` path and the no-timeout fallback: exit 0 at the budget in each case.
shellcheck clean; go vet/build/test and golangci-lint green;
validate_registry.py ok.

Follow-ups. The city.toml `[[patches.agent]] name = "deacon"` interim patch
that emptied the pre_start must be removed by the operator once this lands and
the city's pin moves — it lives outside this repo, so it cannot be removed
here. gcp-wggn: run_bounded's no-timeout fallback signals only its direct
child, so a killed `git fetch` leaves its ssh behind on stock macOS; bounded
and self-terminating, shared with the reaper, so filed rather than scoped in.
gcp-p1q0: the CI pytest set is already red on the fork base; those failures are
filed there and touch none of the files this commit changes.
